# ---- 763 International Relations and Diplomacy ----
H("763.d.1.A",3,"A student reads about scribes, tax collectors and couriers who ran the ancient Egyptian and Chinese empires. This explores the ancient history of:","public administration",["pop music","space travel","sports medicine"],"Ancient states relied on administrators.")
H("763.d.1.A",4,"Ancient empires such as China used trained officials to:","collect taxes and run the state",["design video games","publish websites","operate airlines"],"Early public administration.")
H("763.d.1.B",3,"A class compares a paper-ledger tax office from the past with a modern online filing system and asks if citizens are better served. This considers whether:","current practices of public administration are improvements",["tools never change","old methods always win","service is unimportant"],"Evaluate modern practice against the past.")
H("763.d.1.B",4,"An example of a modern improvement in public administration is:","online services that cut waiting time",["slower paper forms","lost records","longer lines"],"Technology improves service.")
H("763.d.1.C",3,"Citizens complain that getting a permit means endless forms and many offices. A student explains the term for this system of rules and offices and why people often view it negatively. This explains:","bureaucracy and public perception of it",["a type of music","a farm tool","a sports trophy"],"Bureaucracy means rule-bound administration.")
H("763.d.1.C",4,"A bureaucracy is best described as:","an organization with specialized offices and set rules",["a single ruler","a trade fair","a village festival"],"Hierarchy, rules and specialization.")
H("763.d.1.D",3,"Staff morale drops when a public agency is widely distrusted, and its director adopts a stricter style. This analyzes the effects of:","poor public perception on leadership style",["good weather on leaders","music on managers","lunch on leaders"],"Perception shapes how leaders lead.")
H("763.d.1.D",4,"A leader facing public distrust can rebuild confidence by:","being transparent and responsive",["hiding information","ignoring complaints","avoiding the media"],"Transparency builds trust.")
H("763.d.1.E",3,"A scholar compares the idea that many groups compete for influence with theories about how power shifts to new issues and how technology changes it. This analyzes:","political pluralism and related hypotheses",["pasta cooking","farm markets","sports training"],"Pluralism and related ideas explain power.")
H("763.d.1.E",4,"Political pluralism holds that political power is:","shared among many competing groups",["held by one person","held by one party only","held by no one"],"Many groups compete.")
H("763.d.1.F",3,"A city manager reviews whether departments meet output goals, stay inside the budget and serve citizens well. This recognizes that public management involves evaluating:","productivity, budgets and services",["fashion, colors and sizes","songs, beats and tempos","plants, seeds and soil"],"Management measures results.")
H("763.d.1.F",4,"A key way public managers judge a program's success is by comparing:","results to goals and costs",["slogans to posters","colors to fonts","logos to slogans"],"Compare outcomes to targets.")
H("763.d.1.G",3,"A case study describes an official who steered a contract to a relative. A student explains why this is an ethics problem. This researches and explains:","ethics issues in public administration",["pet care","car repair","weather reports"],"Conflicts of interest are ethical issues.")
H("763.d.1.G",4,"Giving a contract to a relative without competition is an example of:","nepotism and a conflict of interest",["fair bidding","open government","audit success"],"Nepotism undermines fairness.")
H("763.d.2.A",3,"A student charts how two parties and an environmental group view a clean-water bill, noting where they differ. This compares:","points of view of parties and interest groups on important issues",["recipes of different chefs","outfits of different teams","routes of different buses"],"Groups hold different views.")
H("763.d.2.A",4,"Parties and interest groups often differ on issues because they:","represent different values and constituencies",["share identical goals","avoid all debate","have no members"],"Different interests.")
H("763.d.2.B",3,"A teacher explains that without free speech and a free press, citizens could not criticize leaders or learn the facts. This analyzes the importance of:","free speech and press in a democratic society",["free parking","free lunches","free shipping"],"They enable informed self-government.")
H("763.d.2.B",4,"A free press helps a democracy by:","informing citizens and holding leaders accountable",["silencing critics","controlling elections","hiding facts"],"Watchdog role.")
H("763.d.2.C",3,"Students are asked to state and support their own view on a current debate with evidence and respectful language. This expresses a student's point of view on:","an issue of contemporary interest in the field of government",["a favorite snack","a vacation plan","a hair style"],"Informed opinions are supported by evidence.")
H("763.d.2.C",4,"A well-supported opinion on a public issue should include:","clear reasons and evidence",["only volume","personal insults","no facts"],"Reasons and evidence.")
H("763.d.3.A",3,"A timeline traces Westphalia in 1648, the balance-of-power era and the League of Nations. This examines the historical development of:","the international system",["the school calendar","the postal route","the train timetable"],"The system of states evolved.")
H("763.d.3.A",4,"The Peace of Westphalia (1648) is often seen as a start of the modern system of:","sovereign nation-states",["empires with no borders","global corporations","city leagues only"],"Westphalian sovereignty.")
H("763.d.3.B",3,"A chart contrasts a Europe-centered balance of power, the period between the world wars and the Cold War's two blocs. This compares:","the classical, transitional and bipolar international systems",["three cooking styles","three art styles","three clothing sizes"],"Systems differ in who holds power.")
H("763.d.3.B",4,"During the Cold War, the international system was mainly shaped by:","two superpowers, the U.S. and the Soviet Union",["many equal powers","one world government","no major powers"],"Bipolar system.")
H("763.d.3.C",3,"A class studies how states, alliances and international bodies each act when a crisis erupts and how they respond to each other. This researches:","national actors and international interactions",["local weather","family trees","sports scores"],"Actors interact.")
H("763.d.3.C",4,"In international relations, a nation-state is an example of a:","state actor",["private hobby","weather system","trade fair"],"States are primary actors.")
H("763.d.3.D",3,"A student explains that leaders weigh costs and benefits, rank goals and pick the option with the best expected result. This examines the:","rational actor model",["random walk model","farm model","fashion model"],"Rational choice weighs options.")
H("763.d.3.D",4,"The rational actor model assumes that a government:","acts to maximize its goals after weighing options",["acts without thought","acts only by chance","never chooses"],"Goal-driven choice.")
H("763.d.3.E",3,"When a border dispute flares, a country debates talks, sanctions, alliances or force before choosing. This analyzes what a nation-state does when:","faced with a problem that requires resolution",["there is no problem","it is on vacation","it has no leaders"],"States choose among tools.")
H("763.d.3.E",4,"Before resorting to force, a country usually tries tools such as:","diplomacy and economic pressure",["only silence","ignoring issues","closing borders to all"],"Escalation ladder.")
H("763.d.3.F",3,"A student asks whether it is right for a nation to back a friendly dictator for strategic gain. This makes observations about:","ethics in foreign policy",["ethics in cooking","ethics in gaming","ethics in fashion"],"Foreign policy raises ethical questions.")
H("763.d.3.F",4,"An ethical dilemma in foreign policy arises when a country must balance:","national interests with human rights",["cost with color","mood with lunch","speed with fonts"],"Interests versus values.")
H("763.d.3.G",3,"Cold War choices such as arming rebel groups raised questions of right and wrong versus strategy. This draws conclusions about:","the role of morality in decision making",["the role of fashion","the role of weather","the role of music"],"Morality competes with strategy.")
H("763.d.3.G",4,"Debating whether to support a regime that violates rights for strategic reasons shows tension between:","morality and national interest",["breakfast and lunch","rain and sun","art and sport"],"Principle versus pragmatism.")
H("763.d.4.A",3,"In ancient times, rulers sent messengers to other kingdoms and today countries keep standing embassies. This compares:","ancient emissaries with current embassies",["ancient pottery with modern pottery","old roads with new roads","old songs with new songs"],"Emissaries evolved into embassies.")
H("763.d.4.A",4,"A key difference between ancient emissaries and modern embassies is that embassies are:","permanent offices with a staff",["one-time visits","unofficial","unprotected"],"Permanent missions.")
H("763.d.4.B",3,"An ambassador represents the president abroad while an embassy staff handles visas, trade and reports. This identifies:","embassy and ambassador roles in international relations",["bakery roles","teacher roles","bus driver roles"],"Embassies support diplomacy.")
H("763.d.4.B",4,"The chief representative of a country to another country is the:","ambassador",["senator","governor","mayor"],"Ambassadors lead missions.")
H("763.d.4.C",3,"A guide lists talks held openly, talks held in private, talks among many states and talks between two. This distinguishes types of diplomacy such as:","public versus secret, multilateral versus bilateral",["hot versus cold drinks","fast versus slow cars","big versus small shoes"],"Diplomacy takes different forms.")
H("763.d.4.C",4,"Talks involving just two countries are called:","bilateral diplomacy",["multilateral diplomacy","secret diplomacy only","public relations"],"Bilateral = two parties.")
H("763.d.4.D",3,"Two countries negotiate a fishing treaty, each weighing what the other might concede. A student models the choices using the idea of strategic interaction. This uses:","bargaining and game theory to solve problems",["baking and decorating","racing and jumping","painting and singing"],"Game theory models choices.")
H("763.d.4.D",4,"In game theory, a player chooses strategies by considering:","what other players are likely to do",["only the weather","nothing at all","only past meals"],"Strategic thinking.")
H("763.d.4.E",3,"When talks fail, a national government may deploy its military while a state guard supports local emergencies. This recognizes national versus state approaches to:","armed force when diplomacy breaks down",["tax forms","school lunches","road signs"],"Armed force follows constitutional lines.")
H("763.d.4.E",4,"In the U.S., the power to wage war abroad rests mainly with the:","national government",["school boards","county fairs","city councils"],"Federal responsibility.")
H("763.d.4.F",3,"A scholar describes naval blockades and troop buildups short of fighting, then studies why wars begin and what they cost. This analyzes:","force without war, causes of war and consequences of war",["music without words","art without color","food without taste"],"Coercion, causes, consequences.")
H("763.d.4.F",4,"A blockade or troop buildup used to pressure another state without fighting is an example of:","force without war",["total war","a peace treaty","a trade fair"],"Coercive diplomacy.")
H("763.d.4.G",3,"Treaties, customary rules and refugee conventions guide how nations treat each other's citizens and borders. This analyzes the role of:","international law in treaties, customs, immigration and human rights",["comic books in schools","crop circles in farms","pop charts in radio"],"International law structures behavior.")
H("763.d.4.G",4,"A treaty is a:","formal written agreement between nations",["private diary","local ordinance","tax form"],"Treaties bind states.")
H("763.d.5.A",3,"A student lists the United Nations and NATO as groups of governments, and the Red Cross and Doctors Without Borders as groups of private volunteers. This identifies:","governmental and non-governmental international organizations",["clubs at school","local stores","city parks"],"IGOs and NGOs.")
H("763.d.5.A",4,"A non-governmental organization (NGO) is one that:","is not run by a government",["is run by the army","is a state agency","sets national laws"],"NGOs are independent.")
H("763.d.5.B",3,"A report explains how the UN Security Council votes, how the General Assembly debates and how agencies deliver aid. This explores the methods and function of:","international governmental organizations",["school clubs","bake sales","parades"],"IGOs operate through bodies and votes.")
H("763.d.5.B",4,"The United Nations was created mainly to:","promote peace and cooperation among nations",["create a single army","replace national governments","end all trade"],"UN purpose.")
H("763.d.5.C",3,"A team drafts a plan for limiting nuclear stockpiles that includes inspections and treaty steps. This proposes a solution to an international problem such as:","arms control",["sports rivalry","weather forecasts","recipe sharing"],"Arms control needs verification.")
H("763.d.5.C",4,"A realistic proposal for an international problem should include:","who will act, how, and how compliance will be checked",["only a slogan","only a complaint","no timeline"],"Concrete steps and verification.")
H("763.d.6.A",3,"A class examines who can make binding rules, what freedoms people hold and what duties they owe, to judge how well a government works. This examines concepts of:","authority, rights and responsibilities",["lunch, recess and bells","songs, notes and beats","maps, keys and scales"],"Core civic concepts.")
H("763.d.6.A",4,"Authority in government means the:","legitimate power to make and enforce decisions",["right to ignore laws","power to hide","ability to avoid taxes"],"Authority is legitimate power.")
H("763.d.6.B",3,"A student explains that the national government defends the country, manages trade and conducts foreign affairs, while also serving citizens at home. This explains major responsibilities for:","domestic and foreign affairs",["pet shows","bake offs","local games"],"Federal responsibilities.")
H("763.d.6.B",4,"Which responsibility belongs primarily to the national government?","national defense",["trash collection","school lunches","local parks"],"Defense is federal.")
H("763.d.6.C",3,"In a mock summit, students practice listening, asking questions and rephrasing to keep a discussion productive. This practices:","communication techniques for exchanging ideas and debate",["silent reading only","timed running","bubble drawing"],"Good communication supports debate.")
H("763.d.6.C",4,"A good technique for productive debate is to:","restate the other side's point before responding",["interrupt","shout","avoid eye contact"],"Active listening.")
H("763.d.6.D",3,"A student tracks how a global trade pact, a federal rule, a state law and a city ordinance each affect one local business. This interprets the impact of:","politics at several levels on the daily lives of citizens",["comics on weather","songs on laws","sports on rain"],"Politics at every level affects daily life.")
H("763.d.6.D",4,"A city zoning rule can affect a citizen's daily life by:","determining where businesses and homes may be built",["changing the calendar","closing the state","rewriting the constitution"],"Local rules affect daily life.")

# ---- 764 Foreign Service (embassy / consular practicum) ----
H("764.d.1.A",3,"A consular trainee explains visa steps clearly to a visitor in person and then writes a short summary of the discussion. This communicates:","effectively using oral and written skills",["only by gesture","only by text emoji","only by silence"],"Clear speaking and writing are core skills.")
H("764.d.1.A",4,"A good written report for a supervisor should be:","clear, accurate and concise",["vague and long","filled with slang","unorganized"],"Professional writing.")
H("764.d.1.B",3,"Staff in an embassy section share tasks, trade updates and help each other meet a deadline. This demonstrates:","collaboration skills through teamwork",["working alone only","blaming others","avoiding tasks"],"Teamwork gets results.")
H("764.d.1.B",4,"Effective teamwork on a project requires members to:","share information and support one another",["hoard information","work against each other","skip meetings"],"Collaboration.")
H("764.d.1.C",3,"An attaché dresses neatly, arrives on time and speaks respectfully at a formal reception. This demonstrates:","professionalism appropriate for the workplace",["casual behavior","careless dress","rudeness"],"Professional conduct.")
H("764.d.1.C",4,"Professional behavior at an embassy event includes:","courtesy and appropriate dress",["loud complaints","late arrival","ignoring guests"],"Courtesy matters.")
H("764.d.1.D",3,"A clerk completes every assigned filing task on time without being reminded and asks what else is needed. This demonstrates:","a positive, productive work ethic",["a low effort attitude","avoiding responsibility","wasting time"],"Work ethic means dependable effort.")
H("764.d.1.D",4,"A new clerk is handed a list of filing tasks for the day. Which behavior best reflects a strong work ethic?","completing assigned tasks as directed",["leaving early","ignoring directions","doing only easy work"],"Follow through.")
H("764.d.1.E",3,"An officer is offered a gift to speed a visa and refuses, reports it and follows all rules. This shows integrity by:","choosing the ethical course and complying with regulations",["accepting the gift","hiding the offer","breaking the rules"],"Integrity means doing right.")
H("764.d.1.E",4,"If someone offers a bribe to a government employee, the ethical response is to:","refuse it and report it",["accept it","keep it secret","ask for more"],"Report bribes.")
H("764.d.1.F",3,"A staffer ranks urgent cables first, follows the daily schedule and meets every deadline. This demonstrates:","time-management skills",["poor planning","procrastination","random order"],"Prioritize and schedule.")
H("764.d.1.F",4,"To manage time well, a worker should first:","prioritize tasks by urgency and importance",["do tasks at random","wait until the end","ignore deadlines"],"Prioritizing.")
H("764.d.2.A",3,"A public affairs officer briefs a foreign ministry on a new U.S. exchange policy and answers questions. This demonstrates the ability to provide:","host-country officials with information on U.S. policy",["tourist maps only","recipes only","sports scores only"],"Diplomats inform host governments.")
H("764.d.2.A",4,"When briefing host-country officials, a diplomat should be:","accurate and clear about U.S. positions",["vague","misleading","silent"],"Accuracy builds trust.")
H("764.d.2.B",3,"A program office plans to bring student leaders from another country to the U.S. so they can learn about its culture. This demonstrates an understanding of:","organizing exchange programs",["closing programs","cancelling visits","ending contact"],"Exchanges build understanding.")
H("764.d.2.B",4,"A main purpose of an exchange program is to:","build mutual understanding between peoples",["increase tariffs","reduce contact","spread rumors"],"Exchanges promote goodwill.")
H("764.d.2.C",3,"Analysts review whether U.S. aid for schools in a partner country improved enrollment and goodwill. This analyzes the effectiveness of:","foreign support programs and other U.S. efforts",["school fundraisers","bake sales","local contests"],"Evaluate outcomes.")
H("764.d.2.C",4,"To judge whether a foreign aid program works, analysts compare:","results to stated goals",["colors to fonts","slogans to songs","budgets to weather"],"Compare outcomes to goals.")
H("764.d.2.D",3,"At a press briefing, an official explains a new U.S. policy accurately and politely, then answers a reporter's follow-up. This shows how to:","address and respond to media personnel on matters of U.S. policy",["avoid reporters","argue with reporters","ignore questions"],"Respond professionally.")
H("764.d.2.D",4,"When a reporter asks about something the official does not know, the best response is to:","say so and offer to follow up",["guess","make up an answer","walk out"],"Honesty and follow-up.")
H("764.d.2.E",3,"After a diplomatic incident, a spokesperson stays calm, sticks to verified facts and avoids speculation when a reporter pushes. This shows how to respond to media on matters of:","U.S. policy during a sensitive situation",["weekend plans","menu choices","movie ratings"],"Stay factual in sensitive moments.")
H("764.d.2.E",4,"During a sensitive media exchange, a diplomat should avoid:","speculating beyond verified facts",["telling the truth","staying calm","being courteous"],"Avoid speculation.")
H("764.d.3.A",3,"After a U.S. delegation visit, local officials praise the aid but ask for more training support. This describes:","host-country responses to U.S. programs and official visits",["weather reports","sports scores","menu choices"],"Reports capture reactions.")
H("764.d.3.A",4,"A diplomatic report on a visit should include:","how host-country officials and citizens responded",["only travel times","only menu choices","only weather"],"Capture reactions.")
H("764.d.3.B",3,"Staff write a report on how tourists and movies from the U.S. change attitudes and fashion in a host city. This analyzes the impact of:","American travelers and popular culture on a host country",["rain on streets","trains on tracks","roads on maps"],"Culture shapes impressions.")
H("764.d.3.B",4,"American popular culture abroad can influence a host country's:","views of the United States",["time zone","rivers","mountain heights"],"Soft power.")
H("764.d.3.C",3,"After an earthquake, embassy staff review how quickly local authorities responded and what support was requested. This assesses:","the impact of host-country responses to catastrophic events",["last year's festival","a sports game","a school dance"],"Assessments guide aid.")
H("764.d.3.C",4,"After a catastrophe abroad, an embassy's first priority is typically:","the safety and assistance of U.S. citizens",["tourism ads","sports events","trade fairs"],"Protect citizens.")
H("764.d.4.A",3,"A consular officer checks an applicant's purpose of travel, ties to home and documents against U.S. law to decide on a visa. This applies:","immigration laws and regulations to determine eligibility",["school rules","sports rules","menu rules"],"Eligibility follows law.")
H("764.d.4.A",4,"Consular officers decide visa eligibility by applying:","U.S. immigration laws and regulations",["personal feelings","coin tosses","rumors"],"Law governs decisions.")
H("764.d.4.B",3,"A visa is denied because the applicant provided false information on the form. This explains:","grounds for refusal of visas",["grounds for school trips","grounds for sports","grounds for parties"],"Fraud is a ground for refusal.")
H("764.d.4.B",4,"Which is a common ground for refusing a visa?","misrepresenting facts on the application",["listing accurate facts","showing proper papers","paying the fee"],"Misrepresentation.")
H("764.d.4.C",3,"An officer searches treaties, country records and security databases to confirm the rules that apply to an applicant. This researches:","documents and databases on U.S. and host-country laws and regulations",["local recipes","family photos","sports cards"],"Research ensures correct decisions.")
H("764.d.4.C",4,"Before making a ruling, consular staff should consult:","official laws and verified databases",["gossip","guesses","advertisements"],"Use authoritative sources.")
H("764.d.4.D",3,"A staffer checks passports, birth certificates and fingerprints before issuing a travel document. This applies:","identification and documentation procedures",["decorating procedures","cooking procedures","drawing procedures"],"Verify identity carefully.")
H("764.d.4.D",4,"Verifying an identity document is important because it:","prevents fraud and protects security",["wastes time","hides identity","speeds mistakes"],"Verification stops fraud.")
H("764.d.5.A",3,"In a simulation, students negotiate with a mock host government over tax exemptions, reciprocal treatment and diplomatic privileges. This models:","negotiations on reciprocity, taxation and diplomatic privileges",["a card game","a bake sale","a relay race"],"Practice negotiating.")
H("764.d.5.A",4,"Reciprocity in diplomacy means:","each country gives the other equal treatment",["one side always wins","no rules apply","only one side acts"],"Mutual equal treatment.")
H("764.d.5.B",3,"A logistics officer plans how an embassy will buy fuel, furniture and services within budget and rules. This designs a program that:","buys and sells goods and services for diplomatic operations",["sells art at fairs","stocks a school store","runs a lemonade stand"],"Embassies need supply systems.")
H("764.d.5.B",4,"Purchasing for an embassy should follow:","government procurement rules and budgets",["personal preference only","random choice","no records"],"Follow procurement rules.")

